No—pulling in a heavy npm stream-processing library is usually unnecessary for this. Node’s built-in `fs` streams + `readline` already handle large files safely with constant memory, line-by-line backpressure, and good performance.

Here’s a minimal utility that reads a local text log file line-by-line and extracts lines containing `"ERROR"`.

## Node.js utility (no extra deps)

```js
// errorLines.js
import fs from "node:fs";
import readline from "node:readline";

/**
 * Stream a text file line-by-line and collect lines containing `needle`.
 * Constant-memory streaming (except for the returned matches array).
 *
 * @param {string} filePath
 * @param {object} [opts]
 * @param {string|RegExp} [opts.needle="ERROR"] substring or regex to match
 * @param {AbortSignal} [opts.signal] optional abort support
 * @param {(line: string) => void} [opts.onMatch] optional callback per match
 * @returns {Promise<string[]>} matched lines (omit if using onMatch for constant memory)
 */
export async function readErrorLines(filePath, opts = {}) {
  const {
    needle = "ERROR",
    signal,
    onMatch,
  } = opts;

  const input = fs.createReadStream(filePath, { encoding: "utf8" });

  // Ensure stream is closed if aborted
  if (signal) {
    if (signal.aborted) input.destroy(new Error("Aborted"));
    signal.addEventListener("abort", () => input.destroy(new Error("Aborted")), { once: true });
  }

  const rl = readline.createInterface({
    input,
    crlfDelay: Infinity, // handles \n and \r\n
  });

  const matches = [];
  try {
    for await (const line of rl) {
      const hit =
        typeof needle === "string" ? line.includes(needle) : needle.test(line);

      if (hit) {
        if (onMatch) onMatch(line);
        else matches.push(line);
      }
    }
  } finally {
    rl.close();
    input.destroy(); // idempotent; ensures FD is released on early exit/errors
  }

  return matches;
}
```

### Example usage

Collect matches (note: could be large if there are many errors):

```js
import { readErrorLines } from "./errorLines.js";

const errors = await readErrorLines("./app.log");
console.log("ERROR lines:", errors.length);
```

Or keep memory constant by streaming matches to stdout / another file:

```js
import { readErrorLines } from "./errorLines.js";

await readErrorLines("./app.log", {
  onMatch: (line) => process.stdout.write(line + "\n"),
});
```

## Notes (empirical / operational)
- This uses built-in stream backpressure; it won’t load 500MB into memory.
- The only unbounded memory use is if you *store* all matches in an array—use `onMatch` to process incrementally.
- If your logs can contain extremely long single lines (tens/hundreds of MB), `readline` may still struggle; if that’s a real property of your logs, say so and we can switch to chunk scanning.

