### Grounding & Comparative Analysis

Under the Edinburgh Protocol, we reject ungrounded abstractions and prioritize minimalist, local-first architectures. 

| Metric | Approach (a): Single Bun Script | Approach (b): TS Framework + Plugins + Build |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Accidental Complexity** | **Zero.** Direct execution of TypeScript via Bun runtime. | **High.** Requires `tsconfig`, bundler config, package manager overhead, and plugin lifecycle management. |
| **Execution Latency** | **Minimal.** Native HTTP client (`fetch`), no compilation step, instant startup. | **Variable.** Overhead from framework bootstrapping and plugin resolution. |
| **Maintenance Surface** | **1 file + 1 config.** No dependency updates or build pipeline failures. | **Multiple files + dependencies.** Vulnerable to dependency rot and build tool deprecation. |
| **Verification Loop** | **Instant.** Edit and run. | **Slow.** Edit, compile/bundle, run. |

Approach (b) introduces a "plugin system" and "build pipeline" for a single-purpose tool. This is a violation of scope discipline. A plugin system is an ungrounded abstraction when the core requirement is a single HTTP request. 

