Node ships `fs.createReadStream` and `readline` — combined, they give you a backpressured, line-by-line iterator over arbitrarily large files using constant memory. That's the division of labor: the OS handles I/O, the runtime handles buffering, and you write the five lines of glue.

```typescript
import { createReadStream } from "node:fs";
import { createInterface } from "node:readline";
import type { Readable } from "node:stream";

/**
 * Reads a local text file line-by-line, yielding only lines containing `filter`.
 * Uses native streams — constant memory regardless of file size.
 */
async function* filterLines(
  filePath: string,
  filter: string,
  encoding: BufferEncoding = "utf-8",
): AsyncGenerator<string> {
  const stream: Readable = createReadStream(filePath, { encoding });
  const rl = createInterface({ input: stream, crlfDelay: Infinity });

  for await (const line of rl) {
    if (line.includes(filter)) {
      yield line;
    }
  }
}

// ── Usage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
async function main(): Promise<void> {
  const logPath = "./app.log";
  let count = 0;

  for await (const errorLine of filterLines(logPath, "ERROR")) {
    count++;
    // Process the line — write to stdout, accumulate, whatever.
    console.log(errorLine);
  }

  console.log(`\n${count} ERROR line(s) found.`);
}

main().catch((err) => {
  console.error("Fatal:", err);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

**Why this is sufficient and superior to an npm dependency:**

| Concern | Native solution | Heavy npm library |
|---|---|---|
| Memory | Constant — reads one line at a time | Depends on library internals |
| Backpressure | Automatic via `Readable` + `readline` | Depends on library |
| Install size | 0 bytes | Tens to hundreds of KB + transitive deps |
| Supply chain | Zero attack surface | New dependency tree to audit |
| Edge cases (crlf/lf) | `crlfDelay: Infinity` handles mixed line endings | Varies |
| 500MB file | Trivial — streams don't care about file size | Same, but with more indirection |

The only thing to be mindful of: if you *accumulate* all matching lines into an array rather than processing them as you iterate, you defeat the constant-memory guarantee. Stream in, process, move on. That's the discipline.

