# The Simplest Fix: WAL Mode + Busy Timeout

```typescript
import Database from "better-sqlite3";

const db = new Database("app.db");

// Enable WAL mode
db.pragma("journal_mode = WAL");

// Set busy timeout to 5000ms
db.pragma("busy_timeout = 5000");

export default db;
```

That's it. One-line pragma each, applied once at startup.

---

## Why This Works

**The Problem:**
SQLite uses a single writer model. When your background sync holds a write lock and a read tries to acquire the lock simultaneously, SQLite returns `SQLITE_BUSY` immediately by default. Your app crashes or retries fail.

**WAL Mode (Write-Ahead Logging):**
- Separates reads from writes at the filesystem level
- Readers see the last committed snapshot; writers append to a separate WAL file
- **Result:** reads and writes no longer block each other
- Single-user app? WAL has zero downside and massive upside here

**Busy Timeout:**
- Tells SQLite: "if you hit contention, retry for N milliseconds instead of failing instantly"
- 5000ms is conservative; adjust down if you want faster failure signals
- Acts as a safety net for edge cases WAL doesn't eliminate (e.g., concurrent writes)

---

## Why Not Other Approaches

| Approach | Why Not |
|----------|---------|
| Connection pooler | Overkill for single-user; adds complexity you don't need |
| Retry logic in app code | Fragile; doesn't fix the root cause |
| Mutex around DB calls | Serializes everything; defeats async |
| Switch databases | You said no; also wrong problem |

---

## Verification

Test it:

```typescript
import Database from "better-sqlite3";

const db = new Database(":memory:");
db.pragma("journal_mode = WAL");
db.pragma("busy_timeout = 5000");

// Simulate background sync (write)
const syncWrite = () => {
  const stmt = db.prepare("INSERT INTO test (val) VALUES (?)");
  stmt.run(Date.now());
};

// Simulate read
const read = () => {
  const stmt = db.prepare("SELECT COUNT(*) as cnt FROM test");
  return stmt.get();
};

db.exec("CREATE TABLE test (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, val INTEGER)");

// This should not throw SQLITE_BUSY
Promise.all([
  Promise.resolve(syncWrite()),
  Promise.resolve(read()),
]);
```

