### Why (a) wins for this problem

Your tool has an extremely narrow scope:  
1. Read a config (or take a URL via flag).  
2. `fetch(url)`.  
3. Check status === 200 (or whatever success criteria).  
4. Output the result (JSON, pretty, or exit code).

That is literally a few dozen lines of code. Everything else is accidental complexity.

### Concrete comparison

| Aspect                    | Single Bun script (a)          | TS framework + plugins (b)          | Winner |
|---------------------------|--------------------------------|-------------------------------------|--------|
| Time to working tool      | < 30 minutes                   | 2–10 days                           | a      |
| Runtime dependencies      | None (just Bun)                | npm packages, build artifacts       | a      |
| Cognitive load            | One file, obvious data flow    | Framework conventions, plugin API, build config | a |
| Debugging                 | `console.log`, Bun debugger    | Source maps, framework layers       | a      |
| Config complexity         | JSON + flags or simple schema  | Plugin system, config validation, schemas | a |
| Distribution              | `bun build --target=bun` → one binary or just the .ts file | Complex build, pkg, or heavy binary | a |
| Maintenance in 6 months   | Still one file                 | “Why did I build a framework?”      | a      |
| Extensibility later       | Easy to grow into multiple files or even a small lib | Already over-engineered from day 1  | a      |

